# PATCH: 025-protect-process-memory
#
# PURPOSE: Give the real browser and Firefox-owned Windows child processes a
#          protected process DACL from birth so ordinary same-user dump tools
#          cannot acquire PROCESS_VM_READ or rewrite the descriptor owner/DACL.
#
# SECURITY MODEL:
#   - A single mozglue helper owns the fixed Owner Rights deny mask 0x000C0010.
#   - The main, sandboxed-child, and unsandboxed-child creation paths pass the
#     descriptor at process creation and fail closed if it cannot be built.
#   - SYSTEM retains full access. Enabled SeDebugPrivilege remains a documented
#     Windows bypass.
#   - Query, lifecycle, IPC handle duplication, and conventional injection
#     rights remain available to the ordinary owner.
#
# COMPATIBILITY: PROCESS_DUP_HANDLE must remain allowed because Firefox broker
#                IPC reopens child peers to transfer handles. The stricter
#                0x000C00FA profile prevents normal browser startup.
#
# LIMITATIONS: Existing creator handles are not revoked. A custom same-user
#              tool can use the allowed PROCESS_DUP_HANDLE right to duplicate
#              a target pseudohandle and bypass the memory-read denial. This is
#              a bounded Task Manager/ordinary MiniDumpWriteDump mitigation,
#              not a determined same-user boundary. Privileged, kernel,
#              in-process, and physical-display observation are out of scope.


